  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "If \\(3^{2x}+9=10(3^x),\\) then the value of \\(x^2+1\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\) only",
    "B": "\\(5\\) only",
    "C": "\\(1\\) or \\(5\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(10\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because \\(3^{2x}=(3^x)^2\\), the equation has a quadratic pattern in the repeated quantity \\(3^x\\). Moving everything to one side and factoring gives\n\\[\n(3^x)^2-10(3^x)+9=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n(3^x-1)(3^x-9)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so \\(3^x=1=3^0\\) or \\(3^x=9=3^2\\). Since \\(3^x\\) is strictly increasing for real \\(x\\), these give exactly \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\). Both satisfy the original equation.\n\nConsequently,\n\\[\nx^2+1=0^2+1=1\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nx^2+1=2^2+1=5.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(1\\) or \\(5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
